		<DeletedComponentTemplates />
		<Id>9000</Id>
		<Name>Citrix Hypervisor - Pool and Storage Inventory (Community Add-on)</Name>
		<IsMockTemplate>false</IsMockTemplate>
		<Description>This template complements SolarWinds' own "Citrix Hypervisor" Content Exchange template (63 host- and VM-level performance counters) rather than duplicating it. It adds pool-wide inventory facts that template does not cover: VM power-state counts, default storage repository capacity, snapshot age and count, and orphaned virtual disk detection, plus the host metrics that are worth having beside them (CPU, memory, one named physical interface, uptime, enabled/live status).

Structure follows the shape SolarWinds' own MongoDB 5.0+ (Linux) v2 template exports on 2026.4: each LinuxScript component opens one SSH session and prints several "Message.&lt;Name&gt;: &lt;text&gt;" / "Statistic.&lt;Name&gt;: &lt;value&gt;" pairs, one String and one Numeric DynamicEvidenceColumnSchema entry per name. Five script components replace the sixteen single-metric ones of the previous revision, so a poll costs five SSH sessions instead of fifteen. On any failure a script prints one "Message:" line saying why and exits 1, so a Down component carries its reason.

Credentials: SSH access to a Linux host in the pool (dom0), with permission to run xe. Components documented "Inputs: None" self-discover the pool they are SSH'd into and need no configuration at assignment time; components documented with named inputs prompt for those values (XenServerHostname, InterfaceName) when the component is added to a node, exactly as SolarWinds' own Citrix Hypervisor template's host- and VM-level components do.

See docs/modules/sam-citrix-hypervisor-template.md in the SolarWinds_OrionGuides repository for what in this template is verified against a real export or host and what is not.</Description>
